      <article class="guide-card">
        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync field guide</div>
        <h2>The practical framework</h2>
        <p class="intro">Routing automation succeeds when every qualified lead gets a clear owner, a clear next step, and a measurable response time. This guide connects lifecycle design, fit and intent signals, territory rules, and escalation paths into one operating system.</p>
        <section><h3>Define the moments that matter</h3><p>Lifecycle stages should describe the relationship between the buyer and your business. Lead status can describe the current working state. Keeping those concepts separate lets marketing, sales, and service report on the same customer journey without overwriting each other.</p><ul><li>Define the entry and exit criteria for each lifecycle stage.</li><li>Use lead status for operational follow-up: new, working, connected, qualified, unqualified, or recycled.</li><li>Document who owns the record at each handoff.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Route by fit, intent, and capacity</h3><p>A useful routing model combines who the prospect is, what they are doing, and whether the assigned team can respond. Start with the rules that must never be violated, then layer in scoring and round-robin logic.</p><ul><li>Prioritize territory, product, language, and account ownership rules.</li><li>Use intent signals such as high-value pages, demo requests, and repeated engagement.</li><li>Balance round-robin assignment with rep capacity and out-of-office coverage.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Design the SLA and exception path</h3><p>An SLA is only real if the system can detect a miss and act on it. Create timers, reminders, escalation owners, and a recovery queue. Monitor both speed and quality so automation does not reward low-quality handoffs.</p><ul><li>Stamp the assignment time and first-response time.</li><li>Escalate unworked records before the SLA expires.</li><li>Create a no-owner and no-response queue reviewed every business day.</li></ul></section>
        <section>
          <h3>Implementation checklist</h3>
          <p>Use this checklist to turn the framework into a working next step.</p>
          <div class="guide-checklist"><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Lifecycle and lead status separated</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Routing priority order documented</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Ownership fallback defined</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>SLA timers configured</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Escalation queue monitored</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Recycled-lead workflow tested</div></div></div>
        </section>
        <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> The goal is not to automate every decision. It is to make the correct next action obvious, measurable, and recoverable when an exception occurs.</p></div>
